# This repository is authored on Linux, checked out on Windows, and deployed to
# Linux. Without this file, Git for Windows converts everything to CRLF on
# checkout, and those carriage returns travel back to the EC2 host inside the
# deploy bundle. A backslash line continuation followed by CRLF is a known
# Dockerfile parse failure, and a CRLF in a shell script produces the
# uninformative "$'\r': command not found".
#
# Normalize to LF in the repository, and check out LF for anything that will be
# read by Linux.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell 5.1 is the exception, and the right rule for it is "do not touch".
# deploy.ps1 is authored byte-exact: CRLF line endings, and a UTF-8 BOM, because
# 5.1 reads a BOM-less UTF-8 file as ANSI and that is how a stray character broke
# the deploy script earlier in this project. Marking it -text means git converts
# nothing in either direction, so the checkout is byte-identical everywhere.
# Marking it "text eol=crlf" instead would leave it permanently dirty, because
# the stored blob already contains the CRLFs git would want to normalize away.
*.ps1 -text

# Never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.bundle binary
